# TypeScript Style Rules

## General
- TypeScript strict mode: true
- Node.js >= 18 (ESM modules)
- Return all errors explicitly — no throw for normal control flow
- Prefer `Result<T, E>` pattern over throw/catch for business-logic errors

## Naming
- Interfaces: PascalCase, no `I` prefix
- Types: PascalCase
- Functions: camelCase
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for env vars, camelCase otherwise
- Files: kebab-case (e.g. bm25.ts, token-counter.ts)

## Imports
- ESM only — `"type": "module"` in package.json
- Imports use `.js` extension (even when the source file is `.ts — TypeScript ESM requirement)
- No default exports — named exports only

## Async
- Prefer async/await over `.then()` / `.catch()`
- Do not leave Promises unawaited

## Logging
- Do not use `console.log` in library code (core, opencode, mcp)
- `console.error` is allowed in CLI and entry points
- Never log API keys, tokens, or request/response bodies

## Testing
- Vitest for all tests
- Table-driven tests with `it.each()`
- Do not mock the filesystem — use real temp directories
